{"entity": "Large Language Models", "type": "concept", "description": "Large Language Models (LLMs) are advanced artificial intelligence models specifically designed for natural language processing. They possess the ability to understand and generate human-like text, showcasing impressive capabilities in text generation and comprehension. These models are built on extensive datasets and are composed of a significant number of parameters, which contribute to their effectiveness and efficiency in various applications.\n\nOne of the notable features of LLMs is their role in systems such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation, where they enhance the accuracy and reliability of generated responses. Additionally, LLMs can process and answer questions while employing both parametric and non-parametric knowledge bases. This versatility is further complemented by their growing capability to work with graph-structured data, indicating an evolution in their application scope.\n\nDespite their strengths, Large Language Models face challenges, including the potential for generating outdated knowledge and instances of hallucination, where they may produce responses that lack grounding in factual information. As research progresses, the focus on overcoming these limitations is likely to enhance their utility in practical settings. Overall, LLMs represent a significant advance in the field of artificial intelligence and natural language processing, firmly establishing their relevance in contemporary technological developments.<SEP>Advanced machine learning models designed to understand and generate human-like text, utilized within RAG systems to produce contextually relevant outputs."}
{"entity": "SELF-RAG", "type": "concept", "description": "SELF-RAG is a model that enables arbitrary language models to generate text with reflection tokens by unifying them as next token predictions from an extended model vocabulary, incorporating both retrieval and reflection in its operations.<SEP>SELF-RAG is a framework developed to improve generative models by creating special tokens that allow self-evaluation and adapt during inference.</entity><SEP>A method for tailoring a language model without additional training, focusing on improving output based on retrieved passages and critic tokens.<SEP>A framework that enhances model performance for training and inference, demonstrating effectiveness in generating supported outputs and improving citation precision through a specified method of retrieval and parameter weighting.<SEP>SELF-RAG is a framework designed to enhance the quality and factuality of LLMs through retrieval on demand and self-reflection, enabling tailored model behaviors at test time using reflection tokens.<SEP>SELF-RAG is an analysis method that evaluates how model answers are generated based on retrieved passages or internal memory. It assesses the frequency of accurate answer generation across data segments."}
