{"entity": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation", "type": "concept", "description": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a technological methodology that enhances large language models (LLMs) by integrating information from external databases, thereby improving the accuracy and credibility of generated content. This hybrid architecture combines the components of retrieval and generation, enabling it to overcome the limitations associated with purely generative models. By blending retrieved information with input queries, RAG significantly enhances the quality and relevance of the generated text.\n\nThe approach involves a data-centric workflow that advances beyond traditional methods, employing a sequence of operations typically structured as prepare-then-rewrite-then-retrieve-then-read. This framework is designed to effectively incorporate relevant information into LLMs, leading to more coherent generation and a reduction in factual inaccuracies, commonly referred to as hallucinations. \n\nOver time, Retrieval-Augmented Generation has evolved through various iterations to better accommodate changing user needs and content requirements. It allows for the retrieval of external information, which, when combined with the generative capabilities of a language model, elevates the overall output quality. However, it is important to note that some RAG approaches may retrieve information indiscriminately, potentially impacting the relevance of the information being used.<SEP>A significant evolution in content generation that enhances quality and relevance by integrating a retrieval component within the generation process.<SEP>Retrieval-Augmented Generation is a method that enhances large language models by enabling them to retrieve documents from a database for improved question answering.<SEP>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a framework that integrates user queries with a collection of pertinent documents sourced from an external knowledge database, combining retriev